{"ts": 1789715749.1461802, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Confirmed: **F3(-1) + F32(+1) + F8(+1)** with in-sample Sharpe **0.761** is the best specification found across an exhaustive-ish search of all 40 single features, all pairs built from top performers, and all third-feature additions to the top pair.\n\nNow let me think about the out-of-sample prediction. With n=5000 daily observations (roughly ~20 years of daily data), in-sample Sharpe of 0.761 was found via a search over many specifications (~40 singles + ~40 pairs + ~50 triples \u2248 130 evaluations), so there's meaningful selection bias/overfitting risk. The individual features F3 and F32 showed strong standalone Sharpes (0.510, 0.467) before combination, giving some confidence the combination isn't purely noise-fit, but I should still shrink the OOS estimate substantially given the multiple-comparisons search.\n\nGiven:\n- In-sample Sharpe 0.761 on n=5000 daily obs (~19.6 years), so IS Sharpe std error \u2248 1/sqrt(5000/252) \u2248 0.22 annualized (or sqrt((1+SR\u00b2/2)/T) type calc gives similar ballpark).\n- Selection over ~130 specs inflates the in-sample estimate.\n- Typical shrinkage for such search-based signals: OOS Sharpe often retains roughly 40-60% of IS Sharpe in factor research with modest search depth.\n\nI'll predict a mean around 0.35 with substantial uncertainty (sd \u2248 0.30) to reflect this shrinkage and uncertainty."}
