{"ts": 1789715836.203097, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\nI searched the 40-feature panel systematically:\n\n1. **Single-feature scan**: Evaluated all 40 features individually to find the strongest standalone signals (best absolute Sharpes: F18 = 0.593, F3 = 0.510, F32 = 0.467, F8 = 0.340, F37 = 0.326, F13 = 0.308, F26 = 0.295, F27 = 0.285).\n\n2. **Pairwise combinations**: Combined the top individual signals with correct signs, discovering F3(-1) + F32(+1) = **0.688**, notably higher than any single feature \u2014 suggesting complementary/diversifying information.\n\n3. **Triple search**: Added a third feature to the best pair, testing nearly all remaining candidates. Found the winner: **F3(-1) + F32(+1) + F8(+1) = 0.761** in-sample Sharpe.\n\n4. **Robustness checks**: Verified sign flips, tried alternative triples, and confirmed the max combination size is 3 (attempts at size-4 were rejected).\n\n**Final specification submitted**: `-F3 + F32 + F8` (equal-weight)\n\n**OOS prediction**: mean = 0.35, sd = 0.30\n\nRationale: The in-sample Sharpe of 0.761 came from a search over ~130 specifications, which inflates the estimate via selection bias. However, the strength of the individual components (F3 and F32 each show large standalone Sharpes before combination) gives some confidence this isn't pure noise-fitting. I shrunk the point estimate to roughly 45% of the in-sample value with a wide standard deviation to reflect genuine uncertainty about how much of the in-sample edge will survive out-of-sample."}
